.TH WERKMEISTER-FEATURE-UP 1 "" "werkmeister" "Werkmeister Manual"
.SH NAME
werkmeister\-feature\-up \- Detect services, allocate slot, generate config, start containers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwerkmeister feature up\fR [feature]
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-w, \-\-workspace <path>\fR
Path to directory containing repo checkouts. Default: ~/workspace (override with paths.workspaces_root in ~/.werkmeister/config.yaml).
.TP
\fB\-\-dev\fR
Run the file watchers in cli/library/extension containers, which otherwise idle.
.TP
\fB\-\-service <name>\fR
Start only this service and wait for it, the way the PR gate does — including one behind the on\-demand profile. \fBfeature stop\fR stops it.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The local services come from the workspace's \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR (via \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR), not from a flag.
.PP
\fBWhat it does:\fR
.IP 1. 3
Loads the service registry and port configuration, and stops with a clear message if a secret one of the workspace's services needs is missing
.IP 2. 3
Resolves the workspace (\fB\-\-workspace\fR, else \fBpaths.workspaces_root\fR from the config, else the nearest enclosing repo's parent, else \fB~/workspace/\fR) and reads its local services from the \fBSERVICES=\fR line \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR wrote to the workspace \fB.env\fR
.IP 3. 3
Allocates a slot (1–12) if the feature doesn't already have one
.IP 4. 3
When the workspace has local services, checks the host's memory and CPU budget (\fBwerkmeister budget status\fR). If the feature doesn't fit it waits in a queue and starts when another feature stops. A start that fits goes ahead of a queue head that doesn't, unless that head is a gate's start or has waited 5 minutes. The queue head may stop features whose session is idle or waiting on a person, oldest first, and says which: a service start at once, a plain \fBfeature up\fR after 5 minutes. A feature a person starts may also stop working autonomous ones (\fBbudget.preempt: false\fR turns all of it off). A \fB\-\-service\fR start that has to wait while an autonomous workspace's feature already runs containers stops them first, so it holds nothing while it waits, then starts them again with the new service
.IP 5. 3
Generates per\-feature files in the feature's workspace:
.IP \(bu 6
\fBcompose.yml\fR — Docker Compose stack definition (workspace root)
.IP \(bu 6
\fB.env\fR — Environment variables with resolved service URLs (workspace root)
.IP \(bu 6
\fB<workspace>/.werkmeister/supergraph\-config.yml\fR — GraphQL federation config (only when a service has a GraphQL type: \fBgraphql\-subgraph\fR, \fBgraphql\fR, \fBsubgraph\fR or \fBfederation\fR)
.IP 6. 3
Verifies k8s port\-forwards are healthy (restarts if needed), only when a local service needs the cluster
.IP 7. 3
Starts containers with \fBdocker compose up \-d \-\-build \-\-remove\-orphans\fR
.IP 8. 3
Regenerates \fB~/.werkmeister/infra/proxy/Caddyfile\fR and reloads Caddy (or starts it, when something here is routed)
.IP 9. 3
Warns about routed hostnames that do not resolve to 127.0.0.1 on this machine and prints the \fB/etc/hosts\fR line to add (the command still succeeds)
.PP
\fBOutput example:\fR
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
=== werkmeister up: my\-feature ===

[info] Loading configuration...
[ok]   Allocated slot 1 for 'my\-feature'

  Local services: backend, frontend

[info] Checking k8s port\-forwards...
[info] Starting containers...
[info] Configuring reverse proxy...

=== Feature 'my\-feature' is ready ===

  URL:     https://frontend.local.werkmeister.io
  Cookie:  X\-Feature=my\-feature
  Slot:    1 (ports 11100\-11119)

  Local services:
    * backend \-> localhost:11100
    * frontend \-> localhost:11101

  k8s services: (everything not listed above)

  Useful commands:
    werkmeister feature status            # view all features
    werkmeister feature logs my\-feature    # tail logs
    werkmeister feature test my\-feature    # run Playwright tests
    werkmeister feature down my\-feature    # tear down
.RE
.fi
.PP
\fBIf the workspace lists no services\fR, the command still proceeds — the feature is registered and routes entirely to k8s. To add services later, list them in \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR, rerun \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR, then \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR.
.PP
\fBIf the feature has to wait for capacity\fR, the command exits 75 without starting containers. It prints \fB=== Feature '<name>' is waiting for capacity (position N) ===\fR, who holds the budget, and the head it waits behind. The feature stays queued and starts by itself when room frees, and its session is told to continue. A \fB\-\-service\fR start waits up to 10 minutes, then exits 75 the same way. Exit 75 means no capacity yet, not a failure: rerun the command. \fBwerkmeister budget status\fR shows the queue.
.PP
\fBIf every slot is taken\fR (12 by default, \fBfeature_slots.count\fR), the command exits with an error. Free a slot by running \fBwerkmeister feature down\fR on another feature.
.PP
\fB`\-\-service <name>`\fR starts only that one service, the way the PR gate starts it, and prints \fBStarted <feature>\-<name>\fR.
.PP
\fBAdding a service to a running feature.\fR To run another service locally, add it to the \fBservices\fR field in the workspace's \fB.werkmeister/workspace.yml\fR, rerun \fBwerkmeister workspace init\fR (this also adds the owning repo's worktree if it isn't there yet), then \fBwerkmeister feature up\fR.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBwerkmeister\fR(1)
